    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  This topic covers the actual <span class=\"learn-keyword\">ledger entries</span> needed to record a consignment transaction in the books of the consignor, including how to value unsold stock and treat abnormal losses.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Three Key Accounts Prepared by the Consignor</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Account</th><th>Purpose</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Consignment Account</td><td>Records the cost of goods sent, expenses, sales, commission, and shows the profit or loss on consignment</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Consignee's (Agent's) Account</td><td>A personal account tracking amounts owed by the consignee to the consignor</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Goods Sent on Consignment Account</td><td>Records the value of goods sent out, eventually transferred to the trading account</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Recording Expenses</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Expenses on consignment are split into two types:</p>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li><strong>Consignor's expenses:</strong> e.g., packing, freight, insurance paid before goods leave the consignor — added to the cost of goods for stock valuation</li>\n  <li><strong>Consignee's expenses:</strong> e.g., carriage from port, godown rent, selling expenses paid by the consignee — also added to the total cost for stock valuation, then reimbursed to the consignee</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Valuation of Unsold Stock</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Unsold stock at the end of the period is valued at <strong>cost price plus a proportionate share of all expenses</strong> (both consignor's and consignee's, but only non-selling expenses) incurred up to the point of it being ready for sale. This ensures the stock is valued consistently with the goods that were sold.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Formula: <strong>Value of unsold stock = (Cost of goods sent + Proportionate expenses) × (Unsold units ÷ Total units sent)</strong></p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Treatment of Abnormal Loss</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">When an abnormal loss occurs (e.g., goods destroyed by fire), its value — calculated using the same proportionate method as unsold stock — is removed from the consignment account and charged directly to the profit and loss account, since it should not affect the normal trading profit on consignment.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Simple Worked Example</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">If goods costing ₦100,000 are sent on consignment, and the consignor pays ₦10,000 freight, total cost = ₦110,000 for say 1,000 units. If 800 units are sold and 200 remain unsold, the value of unsold stock = ₦110,000 × (200/1000) = ₦22,000.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Only NON-selling expenses (like freight and insurance) are included when valuing unsold stock. Selling expenses (like advertising or commission) are NOT added, since unsold goods have not yet incurred those costs.\n</div>\n",
