    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">joint venture</span> is a temporary business arrangement between two or more people (co-venturers) who combine resources to carry out a specific business transaction or project, sharing the profit or loss according to an agreed ratio.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Features of a Joint Venture</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>It is formed for a specific, one-off transaction or project (e.g., a construction project, an import deal)</li>\n  <li>It automatically ends once the venture's purpose is completed</li>\n  <li>There is no formal firm name required, and each co-venturer can carry on other separate businesses simultaneously</li>\n  <li>Profit or loss is shared according to an agreed ratio, similar to a partnership</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Joint Venture vs. Partnership</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Joint Venture</th><th>Partnership</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Temporary — ends after the specific project is complete</td><td>Ongoing — continues until dissolved by agreement</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>No firm name is generally required</td><td>Usually operates under a firm name</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Co-venturers can run other independent businesses at the same time</td><td>Partners are usually restricted from competing businesses</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>No separate set of books is compulsory (though it may be kept)</td><td>A full, continuous set of books is always maintained</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Accounting Method 1: Separate Set of Books</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Under this method, a completely separate Joint Venture Account (and Joint Bank Account, if needed) is opened, and both co-venturers record their contributions and transactions in this shared set of books. At the end, profit or loss is calculated and distributed per the agreed ratio.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Accounting Method 2: Memorandum Joint Venture Account Method</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Here, no separate set of books is kept. Instead, each co-venturer records the venture's transactions in their OWN personal books using a \"Joint Venture with [other party]\" account. A separate \"Memorandum Joint Venture Account\" is prepared (not part of the double-entry system) purely to calculate the overall profit or loss, which is then shared and recorded in each co-venturer's own books.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. When to Use Each Method</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">The separate set of books method suits ventures where a joint bank account and shared records are practical. The memorandum method is more common when co-venturers are in different locations and prefer to keep the venture's records within their own individual books.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> A Memorandum Joint Venture Account is NOT part of the double-entry system — it's a memorandum (side calculation) used only to determine total profit or loss, which is then entered into each co-venturer's personal Joint Venture account.\n</div>\n",
