    "topic": "Contract Account",
    "topicCode": "SS3-ACC-04",
    "module": "Contract Accounts",
    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">contract account</span> is used to record the costs and revenue of a large-scale, long-duration job — such as building construction or road building — that typically spans more than one accounting period.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Contract Costing vs. Job Costing</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Contract Costing</th><th>Job Costing</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Used for large-scale, long-duration projects (e.g., building a bridge)</td><td>Used for smaller, shorter-duration jobs (e.g., repairing a machine)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Work is often carried out at the client's site</td><td>Work is usually carried out at the contractor's own premises/workshop</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Costs are directly identifiable with the single large contract</td><td>Costs may be shared across multiple smaller jobs</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Key Terms in Contract Accounts</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Contract price</td><td>The total agreed amount the client will pay for the completed contract</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Work-in-progress</td><td>The value of work completed on the contract but not yet certified or fully paid for, at a given point in time</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Work certified</td><td>The value of work inspected and approved by the client's architect/surveyor as satisfactorily completed</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Progress payment</td><td>Instalment payments made by the client to the contractor as work is certified, rather than waiting until full completion</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Retention money</td><td>A percentage of the certified work value withheld by the client as security, released only after the contract is fully complete and any defects are fixed</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Notional profit</td><td>The estimated profit on an incomplete contract, calculated at a point during the contract, before final completion</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Why Retention Money Matters</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Retention money protects the client by ensuring the contractor has a financial incentive to complete the contract properly and fix any defects, since a portion of payment is only released after satisfactory final completion.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Calculating Profit on Incomplete Contracts</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Since a contract may span several accounting years, an estimate of profit — the <span class=\"learn-keyword\">notional profit</span> — must be calculated for work done so far. A common prudent rule is to transfer only a portion of the notional profit to the profit and loss account (rather than the full amount), retaining the rest as a reserve against possible future losses or unforeseen costs on the remaining work.</p>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">A widely used formula for cash received based on work certified: <strong>Notional profit transferred to P&L = Notional Profit × (Cash received ÷ Work certified) × (2/3)</strong>, though the exact fraction used may vary by convention or exam instruction.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Do not confuse \"work certified\" (approved by the architect/surveyor) with \"work-in-progress\" (all work done so far, whether certified or not) — WAEC often tests this distinction in contract account questions.\n</div>\n",
