    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">departmental account</span> is prepared when a single business is organised into separate departments (e.g., a store with a clothing department, an electronics department, and a groceries department), allowing the profitability of each department to be assessed individually.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Purpose of Departmental Accounts</h3>\n<ul class=\"learn-list\">\n  <li>To determine the profit or loss of each individual department separately</li>\n  <li>To help management identify which departments are performing well and which are underperforming</li>\n  <li>To support decisions such as expanding, reducing, or closing a particular department</li>\n  <li>To evaluate the efficiency of departmental managers and staff</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Allocation vs. Apportionment of Expenses</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Term</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Allocation</td><td>Charging an expense entirely to one specific department, because it relates solely to that department</td><td>Salary of a department's own dedicated sales staff</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Apportionment</td><td>Sharing a common expense across multiple departments using a fair basis, since it cannot be traced to one department alone</td><td>Rent, shared based on floor space occupied by each department</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Common Bases of Apportionment</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Expense</th><th>Common Basis of Apportionment</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Rent and rates</td><td>Floor space occupied by each department</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Lighting expenses</td><td>Floor space or number of light points in each department</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Insurance of stock</td><td>Value of stock held by each department</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>General manager's salary</td><td>Time spent on each department, or an agreed fixed ratio</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Preparing a Departmental Trading, Profit and Loss Account</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Each department's sales, cost of sales, and gross profit are calculated in separate columns. Directly allocable expenses are charged to the specific department, while common expenses are apportioned using a fair basis, to arrive at each department's net profit.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> \"Allocation\" is for costs that belong wholly to ONE department (no calculation needed to split it), while \"apportionment\" is for shared costs that must be divided using a fair basis like floor space or stock value.\n</div>\n",
