    "term": "First Term",
    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  A <span class=\"learn-keyword\">branch account</span> is prepared by a business (head office) that operates one or more branches in different locations, to track and assess each branch's performance and its financial relationship with head office.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Types of Branches</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Dependent branch</td><td>Relies entirely on head office for goods, and typically does not keep a full, independent set of accounting books</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Independent branch</td><td>Operates with more autonomy, keeps its own complete set of books, and may purchase some goods locally, submitting periodic accounts to head office</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Systems of Branch Accounting (for Dependent Branches)</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>System</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Debtors system</td><td>The branch is treated like a debtor of head office; a single Branch Account records goods sent, cash remitted, expenses, and closing balances to determine branch profit</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Stock and debtors system</td><td>Separates the memorandum stock account (tracking goods movement in quantity or at selling price) from the debtors account and adjustment account, giving more detailed control over branch stock</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Final accounts system</td><td>A full trading, profit and loss account and balance sheet are prepared for the branch, similar to preparing accounts for an entire separate business</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. The Debtors System in Detail</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Under the debtors system, the Branch Account is debited with: opening balances (stock, debtors, cash), goods sent to branch, and any expenses paid by head office on the branch's behalf. It is credited with: cash remitted by the branch to head office, goods returned by the branch, and closing balances (stock, debtors, cash) at period end. The balancing figure represents the branch's profit or loss for the period.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Why Branch Accounting Matters</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Branch accounts allow head office to monitor the profitability and financial health of each branch individually, detect discrepancies (such as stock shortages or unauthorised discounts), and make informed decisions about expanding, supporting, or closing specific branches.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Under the debtors system, remember: closing balances (stock, debtors, cash) go on the CREDIT side of the Branch Account, since they represent value still held by the branch at period end, not yet returned to head office as profit.\n</div>\n",
