    "contentHTML": "\n<div class=\"learn-intro\">\n  <span class=\"learn-keyword\">Public sector accounting</span> deals with recording and reporting the financial transactions of government bodies (federal, state, and local governments), which differs in objective and structure from private sector (business) accounting.\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">1. Meaning and Objectives</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Public sector accounting is concerned with the collection, classification, and reporting of financial transactions relating to government activities. Its primary objectives are accountability and stewardship over public funds, ensuring resources are used for the public good, rather than the profit-driven objectives of private businesses.</p>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">2. Sources of Government Revenue</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Source</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Taxation</td><td>Personal income tax, company income tax, value-added tax (VAT)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Non-tax revenue</td><td>Fees, fines, licences, and royalties</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Grants and aid</td><td>Funds received from other governments or international organisations</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Loans</td><td>Borrowing from domestic or international sources</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">3. Types of Government Expenditure</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Type</th><th>Description</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Recurrent expenditure</td><td>Regular, day-to-day spending (salaries, maintenance, running costs) that recurs every budget period</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Capital expenditure</td><td>Spending on long-term infrastructure and development projects (roads, hospitals, schools) that yields benefits over many years</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">4. Public Sector vs. Private Sector Accounting</h3>\n<div class=\"learn-table-wrap\">\n  <table class=\"learn-table\">\n    <thead><tr><th>Public Sector Accounting</th><th>Private Sector Accounting</th></tr></thead>\n    <tbody>\n      <tr><td>Primary aim: accountability and public service, not profit</td><td>Primary aim: profit maximisation for owners/shareholders</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Funded mainly by taxation, grants, and loans</td><td>Funded mainly by owners' capital, loans, and trading revenue</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Uses \"fund accounting\" concepts (e.g., Consolidated Revenue Fund)</td><td>Uses standard financial statements (trading, profit and loss, balance sheet)</td></tr>\n      <tr><td>Budget-focused, following government fiscal year and approved estimates</td><td>Accounting period may vary; not tied to a national budget cycle</td></tr>\n    </tbody>\n  </table>\n</div>\n\n<h3 class=\"learn-subheading\">5. Structure of Government Accounting</h3>\n<p class=\"learn-p\">Government accounts are typically structured around the annual budget, with revenue paid into a Consolidated Revenue Fund, and expenditure authorised through an approved appropriation (budget) act, ensuring funds are spent only as approved by the legislature.</p>\n\n<div class=\"learn-tip-box\">\n  <span class=\"learn-tip-icon\">💡</span>\n  <strong>Exam Tip:</strong> Don't confuse \"recurrent expenditure\" (regular, repeating costs like salaries) with \"capital expenditure\" (one-off, long-term investment like building a hospital) — WAEC frequently tests this classification.\n</div>\n",
